00:00:03.122 --> 00:00:05.040
PRAJAKTA JOSHI:
Welcome to our session.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.470
I'm Prajakta.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:10.720
I'm a product manager
in Cloud networking.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.900
So I'm sure some of
you are Cloud natives.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.600
Some of you are
Enterprise customers.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.740
And one of the interesting
things is when we talk

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.180
to our Enterprise customers,
they say we want to deliver

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:27.490
smart, secure, modern
services, but we cannot.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.102
They think because they
have deployments on-prem,

00:00:30.102 --> 00:00:32.060
or because they think
it's difficult to deliver

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:37.830
global services at scale with
five nines of availability,

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:39.260
or they think that
micro services

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.050
are cool but not for them.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.560
Whether you are an
Enterprise or a Cloud native,

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.270
our goal today is
to show you how

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:51.420
you can deliver smart, secure,
modern services on Google Cloud

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.520
Platform.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.470
So lets get started.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.990
Let's start with smart
application delivery.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:02.660
What smart smarts do our
customers ask us for?

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.819
They say, deliver
our services close

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:07.326
to our users across the globe.

00:01:07.326 --> 00:01:10.310
They say, help us deliver
reliable services with five

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.400
nines of availability.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.650
Scale our services up or down
in line with our traffic.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:20.430
Optimize our services
for latency and cost.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.400
And enable us to deliver
and move our services

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.250
across GCP on-prem and clouds.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:32.500
That is what smart application
delivery is about--

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:38.470
global, reliable, autoscaled,
optimized, and portable.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.300
There are five key
building blocks

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.380
that enable smart
application delivery--

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:47.734
Cloud load balancing, Cloud CDN,
Cloud interconnect, Kubernetes,

00:01:47.734 --> 00:01:48.775
and the Container Engine.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.100
Now, before we start talking
about global load balancing,

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.640
first let's talk about
Google Cloud load balancing.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.680
I'm going to ask you one thing.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.140
Forget everything you
know about load balances.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.220
Let's start fresh.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.690
So we have five types of
load balancing solutions,

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:10.310
and we are going to focus
on global load balancing.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:12.240
So essentially,
HTTP load balancing

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.390
for HTTP traffic and your
HTTP/2 traffic as well.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:20.200
HTTP is load balancing
for HTTPS traffic.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.660
For your non-HTTP traffic, we
have SSL proxy for your TLS

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:29.190
traffic, and then TCP proxy
for your unencrypted traffic.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:32.370
Let's start with global
application delivery.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:34.110
There are numerous
reasons why you

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.480
might want to deliver your
workloads or your services

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.780
globally.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.110
Maybe you want to deliver your
services around the globe.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:45.140
Maybe you need the highest
levels of availability,

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.590
so you want to replicate
your services in all regions

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:49.520
around the world.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.490
Or maybe you want to deliver
your services with low latency

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:54.620
by being close to your users.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.550
This is what Google global load
balancing enables you to do.

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.600
Instantiate your resources
across the globe,

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.780
frontend these with a
single, frontend VIP,

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:07.540
and deliver low
latency, high bandwidth

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.460
connectivity to your users.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:13.600
And with all of this, think of
the Google global load balancer

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:19.580
as having infinite capacity, so
it'll never be a choke point.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.160
Like I said before,
forget everything

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:23.810
you know about load balancers.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:26.060
Let's look at this.

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:29.900
The load balancer to your left
is a traditional load balancer.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:34.010
On the right is the Google
global load balancing.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:35.990
You might ask, where
is the load balancer?

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:38.270
There is really no
load balancer there.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.280
That's exactly the point.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:43.820
The load balancing is all
software and is done by GFEs,

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:46.280
the Google frontends.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.080
The GFEs themselves
are distributed

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.050
around the globe
in Google's POPs

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:53.810
and the load balance in sync
with each other by working

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.960
with other software defined
systems and the global control

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:58.950
plane.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.280
Now, these GFEs
terminate your traffic

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.650
closest to your user and load
balance the user requests

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:08.210
to the closest healthy backend
with available capacity.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.260
So for a minute, just
focus on the gray dots

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.950
that you see on this map.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:20.410
These are Google's POPs, and
they host the Google frontends.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:22.600
This is where your Google
global load balancing

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.090
happens, close to your users.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.160
And then the traffic
gets sent to an instance

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:30.567
in the closest data center
over Google's high quality,

00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:31.900
high performance global network.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.990
Let's look at this technology
through an example.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.920
A key technology that is used
in Google global load balancing

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:44.150
is intelligent anycast.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.724
Basically, as shown
in the example,

00:04:46.724 --> 00:04:49.140
you can deploy your service
backends wherever you want, so

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:50.660
in any of the regions.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:54.410
In this example, you have
them in US west, in Europe,

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.730
and in Asia.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:59.100
But now is the interesting bit--

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.130
what you will notice is
you frontended all of them

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.497
with a single VIP.

00:05:03.497 --> 00:05:05.580
In another cloud provider,
you would have required

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.182
three VIPs, one per region.

00:05:08.182 --> 00:05:10.140
And I want to make an
interesting announcement.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.860
So we have supported global
load balancing for IPv4 clients

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.780
for now close to two years.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:18.330
And today, we're excited to
introduce Google global load

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.650
balancing for IPv6
clients as well.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:25.860
So shown in this example, your
service myapp.com has a single

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:30.540
v6 VIP, 2001:db8::10.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:34.200
And so Maya in
California, Bob in London,

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.460
and Shen in Singapore, they
all can connect to your service

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:44.256
using a single, anycast
VIP, 2001:db8::10.

00:05:44.256 --> 00:05:45.630
The request is
automatically load

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.940
balanced to the
instance closest to them

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:49.320
with available capacity.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.612
So this is a unique
differentiation

00:05:51.612 --> 00:05:53.070
for Google's global
load balancing.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.153
You will not get this in
any other cloud provider.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:00.790
So now that we talked about
Google global load balancing,

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:02.815
one of the key considerations
for any solution

00:06:02.815 --> 00:06:04.457
is performance.

00:06:04.457 --> 00:06:06.790
And so when we launched the
Google global load balancer,

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:10.660
I think about two years back, we
wanted to see how it performs,

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.880
and we threw a lot
of traffic at it.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.460
We stopped at 1 million
requests per second

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:17.500
because it just went
on and on and on,

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.820
handling the
requests seamlessly.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.550
Another great example,
so how many of you

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:25.340
are familiar with Pokemon Go?

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:28.790
How many of you are not
familiar with Pokemon Go?

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:32.630
So Pokemon Go is a great example
of the scale and performance

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:35.630
Google global load
balancing can deliver.

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:38.000
So when the game was
first introduced,

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.060
within 15 minutes of
launching this game,

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:43.790
the player traffic
really surged.

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:48.260
In fact, the traffic was 50x of
the initial target and 10 times

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:50.782
the worst case estimate.

00:06:50.782 --> 00:06:52.240
We obviously needed
to do something

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.180
to handle this traffic,
so Pokemon applications

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:56.885
were moved to containers.

00:06:56.885 --> 00:06:59.590
They were orchestrated
at this massive scale

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:01.630
using Google Container Engine.

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:05.490
And these container backends
were frontended by the HTTPS

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:08.020
load balancing to handle
the massive traffic

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:09.260
that Pokemon Go was seeing.

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:11.760
So all your traffic was actually
being handled by the Google

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:12.660
global load balancer.

00:07:15.272 --> 00:07:17.230
So now we talked about
delivering your services

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:17.770
globally.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:19.590
What next?

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:22.700
The next thing you want to do
is to optimize your services

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:24.980
for latency and cost.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:27.020
And a really great tool
to use for this purpose

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:30.130
is content delivery networks.

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:32.810
Now, what a CDN does is it
caches your site's content

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:36.260
all around the world, speeding
delivery to your users,

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:38.690
while also offloading
your origins.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:41.870
And so Google Cloud platform
has its own native solution

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:43.640
called Cloud CDN.

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:46.550
And we also have seven
interconnect partners.

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:50.630
So essentially what
you have is choice.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:53.410
Just like cloud load
balancing, Cloud CDN

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:58.010
is also delivered using Google's
high performance global network

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:00.470
and the Edge
infrastructure, of course.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:03.320
It uses Google's 80-plus
points of presence

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:05.850
to accelerate content
delivery for your websites

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:07.550
and for your
applications, and these

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:10.260
could be served out of Compute
Engine or out of storage

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:10.760
as well.

00:08:13.604 --> 00:08:15.020
So two interesting
things I wanted

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:16.820
to point out about Cloud CDN.

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:20.000
One, that it is very tightly
integrated with the Google

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.710
Cloud platform.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:24.950
So if you've set up HTTP
or HTTPS load balancing,

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:28.900
you can just enable it
with a single checkbox.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:31.540
The other thing I wanted to
point out, I think most of you

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:34.720
know that Google believes
in TLS everywhere.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.530
So we do not charge extra for
TLS traffic, so for HTTPS.

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:40.840
And so we recommend that
as a default configuration.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:44.815
You will not be paying anything
extra for CDN for using TLS.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:50.050
So we talked earlier about
this tight integration,

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:51.640
and we said you
could enable Cloud

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.800
CDN with a single checkbox.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:57.180
I also wanted to discuss a new
feature that we're introducing.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:00.490
Now, Cloud CDN also
gets its own UI.

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:03.280
You can see on the screen
there is a left navigation

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.260
bar that has Cloud CDN.

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:07.480
So now you can go here,
as well, and configure

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:09.580
all of the advanced
configurations

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:11.920
and special knobs that
you need for your CDN.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:14.020
You're the first
people actually looking

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:17.150
at this snapshot of the screen,
but it just rolled out today.

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:20.370
So please try it out.

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:23.070
Just like cloud load
balancing, performance

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:26.670
is, again, a very key
consideration for Cloud CDNs.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:30.060
How many of you have
heard of Cedexis?

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.380
Great.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:35.660
So Cedexis is essentially
a web traffic optimization

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:38.900
and analytics company, and
they recently published data

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:40.920
on the Cloud CDN performance.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:43.340
So I've included the
link on the slide.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:47.180
I'm just going to walk you
through some of the highlights.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:49.880
As you can see here,
Google Cloud CDN

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:53.240
has much lower latency than
our public cloud competitors.

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:55.110
So we have our
competitor and ours.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:57.440
Ours is the line in green.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:00.880
So lower the latency better.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:02.920
It has much higher
availability as well.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:07.170
So again, you can see the
green line is on the top.

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:11.020
And it definitely has much
higher throughput as well.

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:13.290
So I purposely added
the link to the report

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:16.910
so you can view it offline.

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:18.800
So we talked about
global load balancing,

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.680
and we talked about
optimizing your workloads.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:24.530
But one of the things that
most of you bring to us is--

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:28.400
we have a deployment on-prem,
or we are in two clouds,

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:30.830
and Google is an open cloud.

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:33.620
So the interesting thing about
either migrating or managing

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:37.490
across clouds is that you
require the right set of tools.

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:40.380
We don't have enough time
to go into all of the tools,

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.250
but I did want to talk about
two tools that our customers use

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:45.530
frequently.

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:47.510
One such tool for
multi-cloud automation

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:51.140
that several of our GCP
customers use is Terraform.

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:53.710
It's a tool by HashiCorp.

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:56.210
Now, Terraform has a
declarative config,

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:59.090
and so operators can very
predictably and safely

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:03.320
create, modify, and destroy
production infrastructure

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:04.410
across multiple clouds.

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:07.490
So it's infrastructure
as code, basically.

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:10.420
Another popular tool that we see
a lot of enterprises and cloud

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:12.230
natives use is Spinnaker.

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:14.090
And so if you look at
the sample dashboard,

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:18.650
this is a snippet of a
live dashboard from Waze.

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:20.690
And as you can see
on the dashboard,

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:25.040
Waze is able to manage both
GCP and AWS using Spinnaker

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:26.710
in a single pane of glass.

00:11:26.710 --> 00:11:29.090
And there are many such
tools, so I think just

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:31.670
finding the right set of
tools to manage your migration

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:35.820
and deployment also makes
it much, much easier.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:37.250
But the best
portability actually

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:39.820
comes from making your
workloads portable,

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:41.320
and you can do this
with containers.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:43.100
We have excellent
sessions on containers

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:45.200
throughout this conference.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.202
Once you have your workloads
that are containerized,

00:11:48.202 --> 00:11:49.910
you can orchestrate
them with Kubernetes,

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:54.160
which is our open source
container orchestration tool.

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:56.300
Or you can use Google
Container Engine.

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:58.760
It's essentially our managed
version of Kubernetes

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:00.260
on Google Cloud.

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:02.560
And there's a reason
I put this slide here.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:05.710
If you will notice, there is
a bunch of enterprises in here

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:07.640
and cloud natives as well.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:09.140
And so you can see
that both of them

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:12.470
use Google Container Engine
and Kubernetes for workload

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:13.220
portability.

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:19.180
I highly recommend that you look
at these two services as well.

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:21.700
So we talked about modern,
and we delivered our workloads

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:23.830
across the globe, and
then we optimized them,

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:26.830
and then we took care of
hybrid cloud and multi-cloud.

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:30.010
The next thing is, think of
any of the Google services

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:30.850
that you use.

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:33.422
Let's say Surge
or Gmail or Drive,

00:12:33.422 --> 00:12:35.380
and you automatically
have a secure connection,

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:37.510
or TLS connection, to Google.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:39.400
So just like that
you have a variety

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:44.320
of tools to actually secure your
services in the Google Cloud.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:47.080
I'm going to focus on four
aspects of secure application

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:48.160
delivery.

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:52.540
One, controlled access to
your application instances,

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:56.921
delivering private services,
TLS everywhere, and something

00:12:56.921 --> 00:12:58.795
that's on the top of
your mind, DDoS defense.

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:03.450
You have four building
blocks in here,

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:06.540
so let's walk through them.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:08.930
The first one is essentially
controlling access

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:10.730
to your instances.

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:12.860
This is basically
determining who gets access

00:13:12.860 --> 00:13:16.160
to your instance and who is
denied access to your instance.

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:17.870
A key tool that
you can use here is

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:20.540
the GCP distributed firewall.

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:23.060
So often people ask us,
like, what is distributed?

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:25.820
Or what does it mean
in this context?

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:28.190
Unlike traditional
firewalls, where

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:29.870
you have to actually
take the traffic

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:32.060
and steer it to a metal
box, which often itself

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:34.880
becomes a choke
point, GCP enforces

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.400
the firewall rules on the
application instance itself,

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:40.559
and hence the term distributed.

00:13:40.559 --> 00:13:42.850
The second one is just like
we talked about global load

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:44.040
balancing.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:46.620
A lot of you run micro
services, or services

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:48.660
that you want to
keep private and not

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:50.590
exposed to the internet.

00:13:50.590 --> 00:13:53.670
So we recently introduced
internal load balancing,

00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:56.130
which allows you to run
and scale your services,

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:58.080
or your micro
services, privately.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:13:59.985
And the load
balancing IP address

00:13:59.985 --> 00:14:02.640
is again, RFC 1918
private address.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:04.800
So you can see an
example on the screen

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.950
where you have instances running
in subnet two and subnet three,

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:10.770
and an internal
client from subnet one

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:13.220
is able to access
the service privately

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:14.470
without going to the internet.

00:14:17.727 --> 00:14:20.310
One interesting thing I want to
point out-- so people tell us,

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:23.520
like, you have all of these
tricks under the hood,

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:26.850
and that is true even for
internal load balancing.

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:29.400
Just like other Google global
load balancing flavors,

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.970
the internal load balancing
is not a managed instance

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:35.220
or appliance-based solution.

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:37.110
It is architected
using Andromeda,

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:41.820
so that is our software-defined
network virtualization stack.

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:44.700
And so basically there is
really no internal load balancer

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:47.310
in the path, which is what
you see on the screen.

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:49.365
It can scale as
much as is needed

00:14:49.365 --> 00:14:51.240
to handle your workloads,
so there is, again,

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:54.610
no choke point in your network.

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:56.880
The third thing
is TLS everywhere.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:03.010
So if your traffic is HTTPS, you
can use HTTPS load balancing.

00:15:03.010 --> 00:15:06.250
And for all of your non-HTTPS
traffic which is encrypted,

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:07.930
you can use SSL proxy.

00:15:07.930 --> 00:15:10.630
This is, again, a new flavor we
introduced a few months back.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:12.760
It went GA a few months back.

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:16.480
And both HTTPS load
balancing and SSL proxy

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:18.730
are global load
balancing flavors,

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:22.450
which means you can offload your
TLS traffic for your instances

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:23.530
no matter where they are.

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:26.110
Whichever region they are,
with a single anycast VIP,

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:28.940
just like we talked
about before.

00:15:28.940 --> 00:15:30.940
As I mentioned before,
both cloud load balancing

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:34.000
and Cloud CDN do not charge
extra for TLS traffic.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:38.770
So there no reason you should
not be using TLS traffic.

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:40.910
And then DDoS defense--

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:42.710
so we haven't talked
about it before,

00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:45.020
but I don't know
how many of you know

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:47.570
that when you deploy Google
Cloud load balancing or Cloud

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:52.470
CDN, you actually get implicit
layer three and layer four DDoS

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:53.250
protection.

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:57.240
So things such as against TCP
SYN floods, or IP fragmentation

00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:59.190
attacks, and so on.

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:01.500
You can complement this
further with protection

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:03.235
from our third party
DDoS providers.

00:16:03.235 --> 00:16:04.860
So you see a few of
them on the screen,

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:10.300
like Cloudflare, Fastly,
Akamai, and several others.

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:12.470
And so I wanted to end
the secure part of it

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:14.270
with this blueprint.

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:16.880
I'm not going to go
into a lot of details,

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:20.990
but think of it as protecting
layer with another layer.

00:16:20.990 --> 00:16:23.080
So you have your secure VPC.

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:25.790
You have your secure
hybrid connectivity.

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:28.940
You complement that with third
party virtual appliances.

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:31.010
Then you deploy the Google
global load balancer

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:32.810
to get the DDoS defense.

00:16:32.810 --> 00:16:35.390
And then you finally have
Google's high capacity,

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:37.430
high performance,
global network.

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:40.820
And you get protection even
against UDP-based attacks.

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:42.290
And then all of
this you complement

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:46.100
using the DDoS Defense
from third party providers.

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:47.810
It's just a layered
approach to security

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:50.240
that will keep your
services secure.

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:53.580
So we talked about smart,
and we talked about security.

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:55.910
Now I'd like to invite my
colleague, Anna, to come

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:57.980
and talk about modern services.

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:00.173
Anna, please come up.

00:17:00.173 --> 00:17:03.610
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:07.538 --> 00:17:09.660
ANNA BERENBERG:
Thank you, Prajakta.

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:11.640
My name is Anna
Berenberg, and I'm

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.410
a TL of load
balancing in Google.

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:15.900
And today we're
going to be talking

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:19.079
about modern application
delivery on GCP.

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:21.420
And what does modern mean?

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:26.369
It's modular, optimized,
composable, and open.

00:17:26.369 --> 00:17:30.630
And it's all achievable with
the help of this Google Cloud

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:34.050
and open source solutions.

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:39.090
Let's look at the evolution
of services to microservices.

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:43.330
We can start with the
ancient age of 15,

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:47.660
20 years ago, when we had
a monolithic application

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:51.510
and all the functionality
was built into it.

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:56.230
And as our users started to
demand a lot of functionality,

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:59.740
we naturally split
up location services.

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:03.150
Once we knew how to split,
we split and split more,

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:06.540
until we split
into microservices.

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:08.820
And here we are
today at the stage

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:11.250
of modern
microservices, where we

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:14.010
are saying that the
future of microservices

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:20.430
is containers, serverless,
gRPC for transport layer

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:25.500
to microservices, load balancing
to bind all the microservices

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.690
together to a service.

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:33.950
Google itself spent 15
years running microservices.

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:36.670
There are three basic
building blocks.

00:18:36.670 --> 00:18:39.580
There is a Borg, which
is container management

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:43.960
system at Google, which we
open sourced as Kubernetes

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:46.990
and brought it back
to the Cloud as GKE.

00:18:46.990 --> 00:18:49.570
There is a Stubby, which
is internal transport

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:52.025
layer between open
source as gRPC

00:18:52.025 --> 00:18:55.435
and now is available
in the Cloud.

00:18:55.435 --> 00:18:59.590
And there is a GSLB/GFE, which
are our global load balancing

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:04.450
solution that powers up cloud
load balancing that Prajakta

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:05.400
was talking about.

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:11.760
As you can see,
microservices can

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:15.720
be spread across multiple
environments on Google Cloud.

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:17.700
But if you look
underneath, they're

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:22.530
all powered by a single Google
load balancing infrastructure.

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:25.590
And for some environments,
some environments

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:29.340
use the explicit products,
like cloud load balancing,

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:32.070
and some manage environment,
like App Engine or Cloud

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:36.030
Functions, are using
Google load balancing

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:39.610
infrastructure internally.

00:19:39.610 --> 00:19:42.160
There are four basic
load balancing products,

00:19:42.160 --> 00:19:44.260
and they're going into
different dimensions.

00:19:44.260 --> 00:19:49.270
You can slice them, whether
they're global or regional,

00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:52.480
whether they serve external
or internal traffic,

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:54.370
and different protocols.

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:56.200
More flavors are coming.

00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:59.590
And if you can look here
at backend service API,

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:02.620
this is the API
that actually allows

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:06.630
us to configure microservice.

00:20:06.630 --> 00:20:10.230
Let's look at the microservice
deployment in Google Cloud.

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:14.460
Aside from managed deployments
like App Engine and Google

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:19.620
Functions, we can load at the
Container Engine and Compute

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:20.490
Engine.

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:24.760
And you can see
that on an instance

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:26.610
you can have two
applications, each

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:30.060
with one board and
applications, or the instance

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:33.330
with one application
and multiple boards,

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:39.210
or have GKE pods with
one or many boards.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:42.260
And we can load balance
to microservice endpoints.

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:51.700
We'll be taking example today of
delivering traffic microservice

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:54.880
to the images and
videos of cats and dog.

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:59.270
How many of you
watch videos of cats?

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:00.190
Yay.

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:03.415
According to
tubularinsights.com,

00:21:03.415 --> 00:21:09.430
there were 25 billion
videos watched on YouTube,

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:13.010
and dogs don't even
come anywhere close.

00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:16.630
So with such, we're going
to split the service

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:18.760
into two microservices, right?

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:20.560
One for dogs, one for cats.

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:22.940
And in front of
each microservice,

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:25.360
we'll put a load
balancer, so we can

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:29.060
obstruct the set of
servers that change

00:21:29.060 --> 00:21:33.040
their geolocation, their health,
their number of services,

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:36.750
with just a single
load balanced IP port.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:39.430
We call it VIP.

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:42.030
Even though
microservices are small,

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:43.930
their needs are very large.

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:45.820
But today, due to
time limit, we are

00:21:45.820 --> 00:21:48.400
going to be talking only
about load balancing, auto

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:52.860
scaling, health checking,
and high availability.

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:57.660
Let's talk about modular part
of modern application delivery.

00:21:57.660 --> 00:22:01.410
Besides optimizing
for modularity,

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:06.480
for event driven interfaces,
and improved velocity,

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:11.880
we also want to optimize for
resource utilization, user

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:18.240
and service perceived latency,
and the custom behavior

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:19.830
each microservice can exhibit.

00:22:22.590 --> 00:22:25.840
We already talked before how
to split cats and dogs based

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:28.630
on amount of QPS,
but here you can also

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:33.550
say that if I have a different
set of URLs or names,

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:36.640
I can actually have a
microservice for each endpoint

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:39.260
that can be named.

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:42.680
Or you can split the
microservices by a bottleneck.

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:46.420
Let's say images are
CPU bound, while videos

00:22:46.420 --> 00:22:47.300
are connection bound.

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:55.295
As you can see, not everybody
likes cats and dogs equally.

00:22:58.270 --> 00:23:00.080
So what do we do?

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:04.060
When we try to place
our microservices,

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:06.790
we need to look how to
place microservices as

00:23:06.790 --> 00:23:09.100
close to the user as possible.

00:23:09.100 --> 00:23:09.730
That's one.

00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:11.770
And the second thing,
we can actually

00:23:11.770 --> 00:23:16.630
optimize by placing services
only where they need it.

00:23:16.630 --> 00:23:19.870
For example, there is no need
to place a lot of cats in Asia

00:23:19.870 --> 00:23:23.170
because dogs enjoy a
2.5 to 1 advantage.

00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:28.930
So talking more
about optimization,

00:23:28.930 --> 00:23:32.290
we can optimize
delivery of traffic

00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:35.680
based on the behavior
of different bottleneck

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:36.550
of the services.

00:23:36.550 --> 00:23:39.070
For example, for
the images, we can

00:23:39.070 --> 00:23:42.270
configure load balancing
be arrayed-based,

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:46.240
and the capacity, let's
say, of 1,000 RPS.

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:49.030
While for videos,
they're connection-based,

00:23:49.030 --> 00:23:52.330
and so we will provision,
let's say, 500 connection.

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:54.940
And let's say we have
a set of instances

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:58.620
that share these microservices
on each instance.

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:02.080
What's going to happen
that we will load

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:03.850
balance traffic independently.

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:07.630
So as soon as the
first, let's say,

00:24:07.630 --> 00:24:12.730
the images will reach 1,000
RPS within a single zone,

00:24:12.730 --> 00:24:15.790
the traffic would be
moved away without regards

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:18.190
to how many connections
the videos are receiving

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:19.630
on the same instances.

00:24:19.630 --> 00:24:24.280
So each microservice load
balanced independently.

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:31.300
Or let's say we have a set of
microservices that share a CPU.

00:24:31.300 --> 00:24:34.810
So we want to optimize
CPU resource utilization,

00:24:34.810 --> 00:24:39.040
but we also don't want to
overwhelm CPU with one of the,

00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:41.440
let's say, we have
a jumping cat video,

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:45.160
which is going to
probably use more

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:48.490
CPU than the serving images.

00:24:48.490 --> 00:24:54.580
And so in this case, we
notify Cloud auto scaler

00:24:54.580 --> 00:25:02.230
how to sum up CPU utilization
of all the microservices

00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:05.500
that run on the same instance.

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:09.820
And then auto scaler
will increase or decrease

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:14.350
number of instances based
on common utilization.

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:16.600
Let's say auto scaling
is not enabled.

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:18.850
Let's say you're application
cannot use auto scaler.

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:21.520
Then the load
balancer itself knows

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:26.350
what are the microservices
that share these instances.

00:25:26.350 --> 00:25:33.370
And it will consider
traffic as a sum of traffic

00:25:33.370 --> 00:25:38.020
across all the microservices
within this instance

00:25:38.020 --> 00:25:40.780
and move the traffic away
as soon as it reaches

00:25:40.780 --> 00:25:42.491
configured target utilization.

00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:49.810
So to provide minimal
interruption to a service,

00:25:49.810 --> 00:25:56.720
to traffic, when the instance
is removed or terminated,

00:25:56.720 --> 00:25:58.880
we provide connection draining.

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:02.510
So only new connections
are being delivered

00:26:02.510 --> 00:26:05.255
to non-drained instances.

00:26:05.255 --> 00:26:09.700
The drained instances continue
to serve existing connection

00:26:09.700 --> 00:26:12.290
until configured
timeout expires.

00:26:12.290 --> 00:26:14.147
So if we look here
at the example,

00:26:14.147 --> 00:26:15.605
let's say there
are two instances--

00:26:15.605 --> 00:26:19.010
A and B. And let's
say it's evening,

00:26:19.010 --> 00:26:20.550
and the traffic goes down.

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:24.860
So instance A is sufficient to
serve traffic for both images

00:26:24.860 --> 00:26:26.720
and videos, and
Cloud auto scaler

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:32.660
wants to bring down instance
B. Then in this case,

00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:37.100
it will wait until both
connections are being drained,

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:41.750
and you would imagine that for
videos the connection timeout

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:43.970
will be much longer
than for the images.

00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:47.030
And so it will wait until
both of them drain out,

00:26:47.030 --> 00:26:54.022
and only then it will
take down the instance.

00:26:54.022 --> 00:26:56.990
We provide health checking
for microservices.

00:26:56.990 --> 00:27:00.830
So it's, again, not per
instances, not per GKE board,

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:05.030
but actually per microservice
for each IP board

00:27:05.030 --> 00:27:06.280
that is available.

00:27:06.280 --> 00:27:09.380
Different protocols,
content-based health check,

00:27:09.380 --> 00:27:11.410
and per microservice
configuration,

00:27:11.410 --> 00:27:14.180
where you can configure
different probes,

00:27:14.180 --> 00:27:17.300
different number until
healthy, unhealthy, et cetera.

00:27:20.300 --> 00:27:25.270
Let's look at the example
of composable application

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:26.170
delivery.

00:27:26.170 --> 00:27:27.490
So we talked about--

00:27:27.490 --> 00:27:29.800
first, we split, then we
talked about optimization,

00:27:29.800 --> 00:27:32.680
and now we are talking how
to take the microservices

00:27:32.680 --> 00:27:35.650
and actually make a
service out of it.

00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:39.160
And here is an example of
typical three tier application

00:27:39.160 --> 00:27:43.670
that is spread across different
Google cloud environments,

00:27:43.670 --> 00:27:48.360
and are using also our
Cloud managed services

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:51.670
that are high availability
and where high availability is

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:56.150
actually achieved through
load balanced failover,

00:27:56.150 --> 00:28:00.360
like Cloud Scale.

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:04.530
And that's another example
of a non-tiered architecture.

00:28:04.530 --> 00:28:06.690
I can go a little bit
deeper in this example,

00:28:06.690 --> 00:28:13.500
because let's say somebody
uploads the image of the dog,

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:15.510
and it goes to image services.

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:19.660
Image services will call up
Cloud Functions with a Pub/Sub

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:24.090
to get some special effects.

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:27.280
And then it sends it to machine
learning for breed tagging,

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:31.150
and then for geotagging
to get to know

00:28:31.150 --> 00:28:33.670
where the dog is most popular.

00:28:33.670 --> 00:28:36.930
And then it gets saved,
let's say, in the GCS.

00:28:36.930 --> 00:28:40.540
And once it hits GCS,
it triggers GCS event

00:28:40.540 --> 00:28:41.480
in a Cloud Function.

00:28:41.480 --> 00:28:46.120
And then this Cloud function
produces no less than rotating

00:28:46.120 --> 00:28:47.470
puppy.

00:28:47.470 --> 00:28:53.050
And rotating puppy with
breed tagging and geotagging

00:28:53.050 --> 00:28:57.190
gets saved back into the GCS.

00:28:57.190 --> 00:29:00.940
And so when some user
asks for a puppy,

00:29:00.940 --> 00:29:05.140
it gets a GIF of
rotating puppy, known

00:29:05.140 --> 00:29:08.320
as a bulldog, which
is also known to be

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:10.140
very popular in New York City.

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:18.290
Let's talk about open part of
modern application delivery.

00:29:18.290 --> 00:29:25.130
We use gRPCs open
source transport layer.

00:29:25.130 --> 00:29:27.380
And there are a
lot of advantages

00:29:27.380 --> 00:29:31.190
of using this because it's
based on protocol buffers.

00:29:31.190 --> 00:29:33.670
It allows for
backward compatibility

00:29:33.670 --> 00:29:35.420
and language operability.

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:37.730
And because it has
layered extensions,

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:41.500
it allows for two layer
authentication, load balancing,

00:29:41.500 --> 00:29:42.530
and logging.

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:47.840
And because it allows to send
the service configuration

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:50.210
to client, it
allows service owner

00:29:50.210 --> 00:29:54.020
to control the client settings.

00:29:54.020 --> 00:29:57.860
Typically when we talk about
microservice load balancing,

00:29:57.860 --> 00:30:03.290
we talk about load balancing
type of proxies, right?

00:30:03.290 --> 00:30:08.340
But there are a lot of different
ways of load balanced traffic.

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:10.310
You can have a
proxy in the middle.

00:30:10.310 --> 00:30:11.880
You can have side car.

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:15.650
Or you can have what's called
one arm load balancing.

00:30:15.650 --> 00:30:17.720
One arm load balancing
is interesting

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:21.450
because it's actually
not on data path.

00:30:21.450 --> 00:30:24.380
And so it doesn't
introduce extra latency,

00:30:24.380 --> 00:30:27.770
and it doesn't become a
choke point like Prajakta

00:30:27.770 --> 00:30:29.370
was alluding before.

00:30:29.370 --> 00:30:31.992
So you don't have to
over provision it.

00:30:31.992 --> 00:30:35.200
And so in this situation,
your initial connection

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:36.310
goes to load balancer.

00:30:36.310 --> 00:30:38.000
And then subsequently,
load balance

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:41.240
streams on the background
backend to the client,

00:30:41.240 --> 00:30:45.050
so the clients can talk
to servers directly.

00:30:45.050 --> 00:30:46.540
And the reason I'm
talking about it

00:30:46.540 --> 00:30:51.801
is because at Google that's what
we are using for internal load

00:30:51.801 --> 00:30:52.300
balancing.

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:54.760
We're using one
arm load balancer,

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:59.660
and we're hoping to
bring it soon to Cloud.

00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:03.290
And so to recap
and to summarize,

00:31:03.290 --> 00:31:05.780
modern microservices
on Google Cloud

00:31:05.780 --> 00:31:08.570
benefit for
fine-grained scalability

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:13.070
through load balancing
and auto scaling.

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:15.560
It also allows you
to mix and match

00:31:15.560 --> 00:31:19.190
your services and our
managed cloud services.

00:31:19.190 --> 00:31:23.210
And the future of Compute is
a Container Engine and Cloud

00:31:23.210 --> 00:31:24.470
Function.

00:31:24.470 --> 00:31:25.520
Thank you.

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:28.790
I would like to introduce
Ravi Yedulla, Senior

00:31:28.790 --> 00:31:31.830
Director of the Home Depot.

00:31:31.830 --> 00:31:35.225
