00:33:45.020 --> 00:33:45.830
Great.

00:33:45.830 --> 00:33:48.290
So our journey to the cloud.

00:33:48.290 --> 00:33:50.600
So why cloud native?

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:53.750
Many might actually
look at cloud native

00:33:53.750 --> 00:33:55.370
as a technology decision.

00:33:55.370 --> 00:33:57.860
For us, it was actually
a business decision.

00:33:57.860 --> 00:34:02.870
It was to help us unlock real
business value and agility

00:34:02.870 --> 00:34:07.040
and deliver more features
and benefits to our ever

00:34:07.040 --> 00:34:10.130
increasing expectations of
the e-commerce customers.

00:34:10.130 --> 00:34:13.429
In fact, when I joined Home
Depot in 2010, all we could do

00:34:13.429 --> 00:34:16.550
was basically add an item to the
cart and ship it to your house.

00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:19.070
Today, you can ship the
products to the house,

00:34:19.070 --> 00:34:23.030
you can pick up in the store,
you can ship to the store.

00:34:23.030 --> 00:34:25.370
You can actually deliver
the product to the store.

00:34:25.370 --> 00:34:28.610
You can specify a two hour
time slot to say, hey, deliver

00:34:28.610 --> 00:34:31.639
my product from 10:00
AM to 12:00 PM--

00:34:31.639 --> 00:34:33.679
between 10:00 AM and 12:00 PM.

00:34:33.679 --> 00:34:36.469
And all that requires a really
solid technical architecture

00:34:36.469 --> 00:34:39.530
foundation and the
capability to introduce

00:34:39.530 --> 00:34:41.420
features really at a rapid pace.

00:34:45.780 --> 00:34:48.139
For as many of you
are experiencing

00:34:48.139 --> 00:34:51.920
[INAUDIBLE], like many
enterprises, pre-2012,

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:54.580
we were a large legacy
monolithic platform.

00:34:54.580 --> 00:34:58.320
It was a big platform just
to serve one large team,

00:34:58.320 --> 00:35:00.242
and we were all trying
to make changes.

00:35:00.242 --> 00:35:01.950
And it was difficult
to make the changes,

00:35:01.950 --> 00:35:05.850
because we had to regression
test the entire site.

00:35:05.850 --> 00:35:07.320
As you can see,
the release cycles

00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:10.380
were three months long,
multiple months long,

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:12.579
based on the complexity
of the release cycle.

00:35:12.579 --> 00:35:14.620
And that's where we wanted
to do something about,

00:35:14.620 --> 00:35:17.430
hey, how do we increase
the feature velocity

00:35:17.430 --> 00:35:21.330
so that we can actually deliver
features at a much faster pace?

00:35:21.330 --> 00:35:25.860
2012 to 2016, we actually broke
down our monolithic application

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:28.200
into what I call,
mini monoliths.

00:35:28.200 --> 00:35:32.190
So we broke down our one large
application to between 15

00:35:32.190 --> 00:35:35.070
to 20 different
small applications.

00:35:35.070 --> 00:35:38.250
We realized benefits
pretty soon thereafter.

00:35:38.250 --> 00:35:41.430
Some teams were able to
increase their feature velocity

00:35:41.430 --> 00:35:43.327
from three months to a month.

00:35:43.327 --> 00:35:44.910
There are partially
independent teams,

00:35:44.910 --> 00:35:47.340
not all teams
became independent.

00:35:47.340 --> 00:35:50.820
Some teams like Surge are
still locator, still finder,

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:54.080
because they were not part of
the core e-commerce bypath.

00:35:54.080 --> 00:35:56.700
They were able to push out
features at a faster pace.

00:35:56.700 --> 00:35:59.940
And as you can see,
some teams actually

00:35:59.940 --> 00:36:02.680
were releasing between
two weeks to one month.

00:36:02.680 --> 00:36:04.050
But it was not good enough.

00:36:04.050 --> 00:36:05.640
If you wanted to
roll out something

00:36:05.640 --> 00:36:09.180
like pick up in store,
you have to go all the way

00:36:09.180 --> 00:36:12.150
from your product page
to search results,

00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:16.450
search results to PLP to
product page and the cart.

00:36:16.450 --> 00:36:19.290
How do you actually
decompose all those team

00:36:19.290 --> 00:36:22.330
so that they can deliver
at a much faster pace?

00:36:22.330 --> 00:36:28.210
So in late 2015, we decided
to go completely cloud native,

00:36:28.210 --> 00:36:31.650
and that's where you
can see our site is now

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:34.920
broken up into about 80
microservices and growing.

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:37.830
We expect to top around
120 microservices

00:36:37.830 --> 00:36:39.420
when it is completely done.

00:36:39.420 --> 00:36:42.450
And now teams are able to
actually release between a day

00:36:42.450 --> 00:36:46.200
to a week, depending on
their velocity and pace.

00:36:46.200 --> 00:36:49.650
They're also mostly independent
teams, and that's the key.

00:36:49.650 --> 00:36:51.180
Going to a microservice
architecture

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:53.910
is not about actually
decomposing the application.

00:36:53.910 --> 00:36:56.040
It's actually enabling
the application teams

00:36:56.040 --> 00:36:57.730
to be completely independent.

00:36:57.730 --> 00:36:59.670
If your teams are
not independent,

00:36:59.670 --> 00:37:01.440
it's actually worse off.

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:04.380
All you have created is
actually a distributed monolith.

00:37:04.380 --> 00:37:06.560
You made your
architecture more complex,

00:37:06.560 --> 00:37:09.330
but you created many
more communication paths

00:37:09.330 --> 00:37:10.860
and dependencies across teams.

00:37:10.860 --> 00:37:14.090
So make sure as part of your
journey, if you're doing it,

00:37:14.090 --> 00:37:15.300
pass your test.

00:37:15.300 --> 00:37:17.600
Are your teams really
independent or not?

00:37:20.859 --> 00:37:22.400
So when we started
the cloud journey,

00:37:22.400 --> 00:37:24.190
we wanted to make
sure that we have

00:37:24.190 --> 00:37:29.190
core principles defined as part
of our moving to public cloud.

00:37:29.190 --> 00:37:32.660
And as you can imagine, as
being a Fortune 25 company,

00:37:32.660 --> 00:37:36.590
dealing with customer
data and PCI security

00:37:36.590 --> 00:37:39.282
is at the forefront
and the first thing

00:37:39.282 --> 00:37:41.240
that we want to make sure
that we get it right.

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:44.360
So we want to make sure
that we have the right tools

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:47.780
and processes in place to
protect our customer data.

00:37:47.780 --> 00:37:52.800
Legacy homedepot.com actually
ran out of single data center.

00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:55.540
If the data center
had any issues,

00:37:55.540 --> 00:37:58.520
you would have site downtime,
and customers would not

00:37:58.520 --> 00:38:01.880
be able to buy products or
actually research products

00:38:01.880 --> 00:38:03.080
to buy in the store.

00:38:03.080 --> 00:38:05.690
So one of the biggest
things that we set out to do

00:38:05.690 --> 00:38:09.770
was we have to have multi
data center or multi region

00:38:09.770 --> 00:38:11.346
high availability.

00:38:11.346 --> 00:38:13.220
And then the third thing
that we wanted to do

00:38:13.220 --> 00:38:15.370
was immutable infrastructure.

00:38:15.370 --> 00:38:18.260
The legacy
homedepot.com platform,

00:38:18.260 --> 00:38:22.040
we have some automation built
in, but most of the automation

00:38:22.040 --> 00:38:25.280
was around starting and
stopping the servers,

00:38:25.280 --> 00:38:27.110
taking servers offline.

00:38:27.110 --> 00:38:29.870
But any configuration
changes were made directly

00:38:29.870 --> 00:38:31.040
on the physical host.

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:34.100
So we didn't treat our
infrastructure as code.

00:38:34.100 --> 00:38:37.020
And so if you had to
recreate an environment,

00:38:37.020 --> 00:38:40.070
it took us weeks and
maybe up to a month

00:38:40.070 --> 00:38:43.250
to replicate the
environments cleanly.

00:38:43.250 --> 00:38:44.757
The repeatability was lost.

00:38:44.757 --> 00:38:46.340
So we wanted to make
sure that we have

00:38:46.340 --> 00:38:49.160
repeatability from the get go.

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:51.200
And that ties well
into the next one--

00:38:51.200 --> 00:38:55.160
to achieve repeatability
you need automation.

00:38:55.160 --> 00:38:57.830
With automation, you would
treat infrastructure as code.

00:38:57.830 --> 00:39:01.640
And that way any time you want
to spin up a new environment,

00:39:01.640 --> 00:39:03.980
you just execute your
automation scripts.

00:39:03.980 --> 00:39:07.310
You want to make any changes,
you spin up new environment.

00:39:07.310 --> 00:39:10.430
Don't make changes to the
existing infrastructure

00:39:10.430 --> 00:39:13.230
that you just provisioned.

00:39:13.230 --> 00:39:16.520
And last but not least, as
part of the microservice

00:39:16.520 --> 00:39:18.210
architecture, or
when you're going

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:21.660
from one big monolithic
database to maybe 10

00:39:21.660 --> 00:39:25.820
different databases, from
10 servers or 20 servers

00:39:25.820 --> 00:39:28.470
to hundreds of small
servers, if you

00:39:28.470 --> 00:39:31.800
don't have the right monitoring
and reliability engineering

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:34.260
principles, it becomes chaotic.

00:39:34.260 --> 00:39:36.360
Because you don't
know what went wrong.

00:39:36.360 --> 00:39:39.690
You'll just spin your wheels
trying to troubleshoot.

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:41.820
So you want to
make sure that you

00:39:41.820 --> 00:39:45.030
have the right team and the
right policies in place that

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:48.450
enable the right monitoring
and reliability engineering

00:39:48.450 --> 00:39:50.604
principles for us to
be successful as part

00:39:50.604 --> 00:39:51.270
of this journey.

00:39:54.390 --> 00:39:59.280
So I want to just jump
into the architecture

00:39:59.280 --> 00:40:01.080
that we have in the cloud today.

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:03.210
As you can imagine,
homedepot.com

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:06.230
has a large
technology footprint.

00:40:06.230 --> 00:40:07.920
We have 2,000 stores.

00:40:07.920 --> 00:40:13.439
We have a lot of master data
that resides inside Home Depot.

00:40:13.439 --> 00:40:14.980
We can't move all
of it to the cloud,

00:40:14.980 --> 00:40:18.240
so we'll always be
in hybrid cloud mode.

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:22.840
I highly doubt we'll ever be
completely all into the cloud.

00:40:22.840 --> 00:40:25.870
So we need to find ways
to move the master data--

00:40:25.870 --> 00:40:29.430
whether it is product
catalog, images, metadata--

00:40:29.430 --> 00:40:30.870
securely to the cloud.

00:40:30.870 --> 00:40:34.470
And we closely partnered
with Google Cloud engineers,

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:37.410
used the Google Cloud
Interconnect product

00:40:37.410 --> 00:40:41.320
and VPN to move the data
securely to the cloud.

00:40:41.320 --> 00:40:43.127
And as you can see on
the right hand side,

00:40:43.127 --> 00:40:44.585
we use all these
security features,

00:40:44.585 --> 00:40:47.390
whether it is security
tags, firewall rules,

00:40:47.390 --> 00:40:49.020
the right IM
policies to make sure

00:40:49.020 --> 00:40:52.410
that the data lands
securely in the cloud.

00:40:52.410 --> 00:40:57.810
The path between the Home Depot
data centers and Google Cloud

00:40:57.810 --> 00:41:00.030
is multi geo-redundant.

00:41:00.030 --> 00:41:02.521
We actually go over
three different paths.

00:41:02.521 --> 00:41:04.770
One would be our east coast,
one would be our Midwest,

00:41:04.770 --> 00:41:05.790
and one our West Coast.

00:41:05.790 --> 00:41:09.210
So if any time any
path is unavailable,

00:41:09.210 --> 00:41:12.510
we have a path back
into Google Cloud.

00:41:12.510 --> 00:41:16.560
And the next one is
basically, as you can see,

00:41:16.560 --> 00:41:19.640
the migration of homedepot.com
into Google Cloud

00:41:19.640 --> 00:41:21.750
is not a big bang approach.

00:41:21.750 --> 00:41:25.970
We migrated parts of it
over long periods of time.

00:41:25.970 --> 00:41:29.010
And for that you might
have a shopping cart

00:41:29.010 --> 00:41:30.740
running inside the
legacy shopping cart,

00:41:30.740 --> 00:41:32.700
running in Home
Depot data centers,

00:41:32.700 --> 00:41:34.470
and trying to talk
to a promotion

00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:38.290
API in Google Cloud
and vice versa.

00:41:38.290 --> 00:41:41.570
You have shopping cart in Google
Cloud, talking to an inventory

00:41:41.570 --> 00:41:44.020
API inside the Home
Depot, because inventory

00:41:44.020 --> 00:41:47.340
is global between
stores and online.

00:41:47.340 --> 00:41:50.520
And to enable secure
communication between the two,

00:41:50.520 --> 00:41:53.800
we worked closely
with Google engineers,

00:41:53.800 --> 00:41:56.580
used the API
gateway on our side,

00:41:56.580 --> 00:41:58.740
going through the
same interconnect path

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:02.040
to make sure that the data
is securely transferred

00:42:02.040 --> 00:42:03.160
between the two locations.

00:42:06.580 --> 00:42:09.840
Multi-region high availability--
as I said, from the

00:42:09.840 --> 00:42:11.760
get go we wanted to
make sure that we

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:13.500
run across multiple regions.

00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:14.670
So this was not--

00:42:14.670 --> 00:42:17.430
homedepot.com didn't start
out just running in one region

00:42:17.430 --> 00:42:20.040
and then added another
reason as built on.

00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:24.000
We started out with US east
and US central from the get go.

00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:28.530
Here I just show two
zones in each region.

00:42:28.530 --> 00:42:31.290
We actually run out of
three zones in each.

00:42:31.290 --> 00:42:34.290
The two zones here are
just for clarity purposes.

00:42:34.290 --> 00:42:37.170
So we have three zones in US
central and three zones in US

00:42:37.170 --> 00:42:38.130
east.

00:42:38.130 --> 00:42:40.050
All the databases are
actually replicated

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:41.470
across all six zones.

00:42:41.470 --> 00:42:45.510
So we have three copies in US
central, three copies in US

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:51.970
east, and we also make a
backup every day back into GCS.

00:42:51.970 --> 00:42:55.210
This is actually one of
the strongest features

00:42:55.210 --> 00:43:00.072
for choosing Google Cloud.

00:43:00.072 --> 00:43:04.900
In Google Cloud,
VPC, our project,

00:43:04.900 --> 00:43:07.210
all regions are
globally visible.

00:43:07.210 --> 00:43:11.260
Basically the VMs are globally
visible across US east and US

00:43:11.260 --> 00:43:15.220
central, and setting up an
application is extremely easy.

00:43:15.220 --> 00:43:16.720
In other cloud
providers, you have

00:43:16.720 --> 00:43:19.617
to go through a myriad of
set up paths, between--

00:43:19.617 --> 00:43:21.200
whether it's VPC
peering, whether it's

00:43:21.200 --> 00:43:23.050
VPN, or going over
the public internet

00:43:23.050 --> 00:43:26.650
to transfer or replicate the
data across those two regions.

00:43:26.650 --> 00:43:31.120
So Google Cloud, our network,
and the global visibility

00:43:31.120 --> 00:43:35.950
has really helped us and
made it really simple for us.

00:43:35.950 --> 00:43:40.390
And on the frontend,
as Prajakta said,

00:43:40.390 --> 00:43:42.400
we use layer seven
load balancer,

00:43:42.400 --> 00:43:44.560
and we have a single global IP.

00:43:44.560 --> 00:43:46.810
The global IP, basically,
is smart enough

00:43:46.810 --> 00:43:50.530
to route the requests to
the closest region based

00:43:50.530 --> 00:43:52.030
on the customer's location.

00:43:52.030 --> 00:43:55.460
So if somebody is visiting
homedepot.com from Washington,

00:43:55.460 --> 00:43:58.046
that request is
routed to US east,

00:43:58.046 --> 00:43:59.170
which is in South Carolina.

00:43:59.170 --> 00:44:03.490
Whereas if somebody was
visiting dotcom from Chicago,

00:44:03.490 --> 00:44:06.670
the request is
routed to US central.

00:44:06.670 --> 00:44:12.490
This also gives us the ability
to actually test DR. As in--

00:44:12.490 --> 00:44:16.360
what happens if
US east goes away?

00:44:16.360 --> 00:44:18.130
Or what happens if
US central goes away?

00:44:18.130 --> 00:44:20.180
We want to make sure
that we can survive

00:44:20.180 --> 00:44:24.080
the loss of a single zone in
a region, or a complete region

00:44:24.080 --> 00:44:27.830
outage, and not
affect our reliability

00:44:27.830 --> 00:44:29.960
or SLO back to our customers.

00:44:29.960 --> 00:44:34.250
So this architecture enabled
us to easily achieve that.

00:44:34.250 --> 00:44:37.530
In the future, if you wanted
to add another region,

00:44:37.530 --> 00:44:39.860
whether it is US
west or the newly

00:44:39.860 --> 00:44:44.220
announced California region,
it becomes very simple.

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:46.760
We don't have to do
any DNS record changes.

00:44:46.760 --> 00:44:50.360
We don't have to make any
changes anywhere else other

00:44:50.360 --> 00:44:52.760
than just adding the
VMs in the right region.

00:44:52.760 --> 00:44:54.980
And the traffic
automatically gets routed,

00:44:54.980 --> 00:44:57.930
and customers benefit from
increased performance.

00:45:01.350 --> 00:45:02.840
As I said, we run
out of six zones.

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:05.310
So what does a zone look like?

00:45:05.310 --> 00:45:09.270
Basically every zone is
its own independent copy

00:45:09.270 --> 00:45:12.000
of the entire website.

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:15.000
As you can see, we
run only on VMs.

00:45:15.000 --> 00:45:17.790
We do not do containers today.

00:45:17.790 --> 00:45:21.280
The main reasons for using
VMs are network isolation,

00:45:21.280 --> 00:45:24.390
segmentation, and
data persistence.

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:27.160
Containers, we
are looking at it.

00:45:27.160 --> 00:45:31.610
Someday maybe we'll look at it.

00:45:31.610 --> 00:45:35.130
Each VM runs only
one microservice,

00:45:35.130 --> 00:45:38.525
and we have smaller VMs
that just scale out.

00:45:38.525 --> 00:45:39.900
As you can see,
basically we have

00:45:39.900 --> 00:45:42.390
clusters of different
services that

00:45:42.390 --> 00:45:44.130
just scale out using
auto scaling based

00:45:44.130 --> 00:45:46.440
on traffic demand.

00:45:46.440 --> 00:45:49.360
Each zone also has a
service registry cluster.

00:45:49.360 --> 00:45:51.840
So when new services
come up, they

00:45:51.840 --> 00:45:54.970
register their services in
the service registry cluster

00:45:54.970 --> 00:45:58.830
and are visible to the
clients in the same zone.

00:45:58.830 --> 00:46:03.044
The load balancing from client
to services in the same zone

00:46:03.044 --> 00:46:04.710
is through a client
side load balancing.

00:46:04.710 --> 00:46:07.740
We don't use internal
load balancer, mostly

00:46:07.740 --> 00:46:10.290
in part because ILB
was not available

00:46:10.290 --> 00:46:14.610
when dotcom went to life.

00:46:14.610 --> 00:46:17.100
Zone is our fault
isolation domain

00:46:17.100 --> 00:46:20.340
so that the blast
radius is contained.

00:46:20.340 --> 00:46:23.970
Any faults or any issues
that we have in one zone

00:46:23.970 --> 00:46:30.900
don't propagate into regional
falls or global falls.

00:46:30.900 --> 00:46:33.210
The other cool thing
that we do is actually,

00:46:33.210 --> 00:46:37.286
basically-- as you can see,
we have databases per service.

00:46:37.286 --> 00:46:38.660
So if a service
needs a database,

00:46:38.660 --> 00:46:40.140
it gets its own database.

00:46:40.140 --> 00:46:43.760
There is no database sharing
between multiple microservices.

00:46:43.760 --> 00:46:45.990
Service A, if it
wants a database,

00:46:45.990 --> 00:46:47.800
it has its own database cluster.

00:46:47.800 --> 00:46:50.870
Service D, whether it's examples
of shipping or promotion,

00:46:50.870 --> 00:46:52.810
would have its own
database cluster.

00:46:52.810 --> 00:46:55.230
And there is network
firewall rules

00:46:55.230 --> 00:46:59.760
that limit the access
between the right service

00:46:59.760 --> 00:47:00.930
to the right database.

00:47:00.930 --> 00:47:04.840
Service A here cannot
access Service D.

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:08.290
The other thing that
we do is our services,

00:47:08.290 --> 00:47:09.970
when they want to
talk to the database,

00:47:09.970 --> 00:47:13.180
the credentials are not stored
in any config files, property

00:47:13.180 --> 00:47:16.060
files, or encrypted
file systems.

00:47:16.060 --> 00:47:20.260
We actually have a secret
registry cluster per zone.

00:47:20.260 --> 00:47:24.460
And whenever any VM comes up,
it talks to our secret history,

00:47:24.460 --> 00:47:27.070
gets the DB credentials
dynamically per VM,

00:47:27.070 --> 00:47:30.670
so every VM gets its
own DB credentials.

00:47:30.670 --> 00:47:33.550
There is no shared DB
credentials across VMs.

00:47:33.550 --> 00:47:40.450
That enables us to contain
any issues that we have

00:47:40.450 --> 00:47:44.110
and be absolutely sure that
the environment is secure.

00:47:47.620 --> 00:47:49.380
Awesome.

00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:54.630
So we began this journey about
15 months ago, learned a lot,

00:47:54.630 --> 00:47:57.210
partnered with Google.

00:47:57.210 --> 00:47:59.250
They helped us learn
quite a few things,

00:47:59.250 --> 00:48:01.670
and I'm sure they
learned a lot from us.

00:48:01.670 --> 00:48:03.150
It's been a great partnership.

00:48:03.150 --> 00:48:04.620
So I just want to
leave you with--

00:48:04.620 --> 00:48:07.275
what are the key lessons
learned as part of this journey?

00:48:07.275 --> 00:48:12.350
We are about 75%
into the migration.

00:48:12.350 --> 00:48:15.210
So about 75% of
homedepot.com traffic

00:48:15.210 --> 00:48:18.090
is now sorted out
of Google, and we

00:48:18.090 --> 00:48:24.230
will finish this in the
next five to six months.

00:48:24.230 --> 00:48:26.547
We did a pretty
good job in actually

00:48:26.547 --> 00:48:28.130
moving into microservice
architecture,

00:48:28.130 --> 00:48:31.220
but we dumped all
our microservices

00:48:31.220 --> 00:48:33.790
into a single GCP Project.

00:48:33.790 --> 00:48:36.240
So we didn't follow
the same principles

00:48:36.240 --> 00:48:38.120
in choosing the project size.

00:48:38.120 --> 00:48:40.310
So GCP project size matters.

00:48:40.310 --> 00:48:44.170
We have hundreds, thousands
of VMs in one GCP project.

00:48:44.170 --> 00:48:47.690
You run into limitations
between instance quarters,

00:48:47.690 --> 00:48:51.590
console becoming really
slow, many people

00:48:51.590 --> 00:48:53.300
working in the same project.

00:48:53.300 --> 00:48:56.310
So flavors-- break
up your GCP projects

00:48:56.310 --> 00:48:57.560
into smaller projects as well.

00:49:00.280 --> 00:49:02.950
As you can see, one of the
biggest benefits of cloud

00:49:02.950 --> 00:49:03.860
is autoscaling.

00:49:03.860 --> 00:49:05.590
So we don't have
to pre-provision

00:49:05.590 --> 00:49:08.980
the infrastructure that
we need for peak demand.

00:49:08.980 --> 00:49:11.380
We leverage autoscaling
quite heavily,

00:49:11.380 --> 00:49:15.040
and GCP autoscaling
works really well.

00:49:15.040 --> 00:49:17.155
The VM instances
spin up in 14 seconds

00:49:17.155 --> 00:49:19.300
and are ready to take traffic.

00:49:19.300 --> 00:49:23.260
But make sure when your
VM instances spin up,

00:49:23.260 --> 00:49:25.460
there are no external
dependencies.

00:49:25.460 --> 00:49:28.120
Some of our applications,
like search,

00:49:28.120 --> 00:49:31.000
they download huge
index files from storage

00:49:31.000 --> 00:49:33.160
when a VM boots up.

00:49:33.160 --> 00:49:37.270
The index file is somewhere
around three to five gigabytes.

00:49:37.270 --> 00:49:39.910
That takes time, and
search engines typically

00:49:39.910 --> 00:49:43.850
need warm up time before
they can sort traffic.

00:49:43.850 --> 00:49:46.720
The way autoscaling is
designed, and the way

00:49:46.720 --> 00:49:50.930
we actually route traffic, as
soon as an instance comes up,

00:49:50.930 --> 00:49:52.680
guest register in
the service registry,

00:49:52.680 --> 00:49:54.250
it's ready to take traffic.

00:49:54.250 --> 00:49:57.820
So we've seen issues where
instances are set up,

00:49:57.820 --> 00:50:00.490
but the performance
actually goes down.

00:50:00.490 --> 00:50:03.670
Because traffic is
routed, and the VMs

00:50:03.670 --> 00:50:05.560
are struggling to
sort the traffic.

00:50:05.560 --> 00:50:08.320
So make sure that the external
dependencies are minimized

00:50:08.320 --> 00:50:11.960
as part of your bootstrap.

00:50:11.960 --> 00:50:15.120
Telemetry-- what's the
telemetry patterns?

00:50:15.120 --> 00:50:18.490
On-prem-- we used
logs to do telemetry.

00:50:18.490 --> 00:50:23.020
So basically logs are used for
HTTP response codes, latency,

00:50:23.020 --> 00:50:24.430
and availability.

00:50:24.430 --> 00:50:27.490
We ported the same
pattern into the cloud,

00:50:27.490 --> 00:50:29.620
and it gave us similar metrics.

00:50:29.620 --> 00:50:32.440
But in Cloud, you want
even more metrics.

00:50:32.440 --> 00:50:37.330
You just don't want the
regular availability latency.

00:50:37.330 --> 00:50:39.730
We want key business metrics.

00:50:39.730 --> 00:50:42.980
You want metrics around, hey,
how much memory am I using?

00:50:42.980 --> 00:50:47.050
How much memory am I using to
serve this particular request?

00:50:47.050 --> 00:50:50.450
Is the CPU low or high on
one segment of requests?

00:50:50.450 --> 00:50:52.210
Those are not
available if you just

00:50:52.210 --> 00:50:54.760
rely on log-based telemetry.

00:50:54.760 --> 00:50:58.800
So we are actually
moving to StatsD.

00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:01.330
Today, the log-based
telemetry is

00:51:01.330 --> 00:51:05.020
through the google-fluentd
and collectd plugins.

00:51:05.020 --> 00:51:07.060
We're going to move to StatsD.

00:51:07.060 --> 00:51:10.850
The application itself uses
the StatsD library, exposes

00:51:10.850 --> 00:51:14.440
whatever metrics they want to
expose directly into StatsD,

00:51:14.440 --> 00:51:18.010
and then StatsD communicates
to the Google monitoring API

00:51:18.010 --> 00:51:20.650
for a different set of metrics.

00:51:20.650 --> 00:51:24.510
And last but not the
least, resiliency--

00:51:24.510 --> 00:51:27.210
when you're running on-prem,
you assume the network

00:51:27.210 --> 00:51:28.200
is going to be stable.

00:51:28.200 --> 00:51:30.850
You assume the file system
is going to be available.

00:51:30.850 --> 00:51:34.860
There's not a whole lot of
cross zone interaction, cross DC

00:51:34.860 --> 00:51:36.260
interactions.

00:51:36.260 --> 00:51:39.480
But in public cloud,
you have-- as I said,

00:51:39.480 --> 00:51:41.729
we run across six zones.

00:51:41.729 --> 00:51:43.270
There's a lot of
network connections,

00:51:43.270 --> 00:51:45.530
a lot of microservices.

00:51:45.530 --> 00:51:48.660
We have cross
location connections.

00:51:48.660 --> 00:51:50.640
Resiliency is key.

00:51:50.640 --> 00:51:53.130
For example, as part
of the migration,

00:51:53.130 --> 00:51:55.530
we had one service in
Google Cloud reaching out

00:51:55.530 --> 00:51:58.840
to, let's say, header
service, basically

00:51:58.840 --> 00:52:01.950
the header bar that shows up
at the top navigation bar.

00:52:01.950 --> 00:52:03.960
That was running on-prem.

00:52:03.960 --> 00:52:08.000
The service always assumed
that the data returned

00:52:08.000 --> 00:52:10.900
is going to be right, or if
the data was not available,

00:52:10.900 --> 00:52:14.400
just show the error
message back to the user.

00:52:14.400 --> 00:52:17.400
And some instances, because
of the network connectivity

00:52:17.400 --> 00:52:21.510
between Google and Home
Depot on-prem data center,

00:52:21.510 --> 00:52:24.180
the data was not returned,
or it was an error.

00:52:24.180 --> 00:52:28.650
And the user saw the page
without the top navigation bar.

00:52:28.650 --> 00:52:31.400
So make sure you have
resiliency built in.

00:52:31.400 --> 00:52:33.900
Don't assume network is
going to be available.

00:52:33.900 --> 00:52:39.990
Don't just assume that, hey,
I connected to a service

00:52:39.990 --> 00:52:43.120
instance, it returned me some
data or returned an error,

00:52:43.120 --> 00:52:44.880
and do not retry.

00:52:44.880 --> 00:52:47.670
Make sure you have some
retry policies built in,

00:52:47.670 --> 00:52:51.210
so that you can show
the right messages

00:52:51.210 --> 00:52:55.420
and have the right resiliency
for your customers.

00:52:55.420 --> 00:52:57.010
And that's it.

00:52:57.010 --> 00:53:01.050
So I'd like to introduce
Prajakta back for the demo.

00:53:01.050 --> 00:53:04.375
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:07.700 --> 00:53:10.240
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So
thank you, Ravi.

00:53:10.240 --> 00:53:12.410
It's always great when
customers come here,

00:53:12.410 --> 00:53:16.510
and then they talk about
they're real pain points,

00:53:16.510 --> 00:53:19.250
and then they talk about
their real world deployments.

00:53:19.250 --> 00:53:20.450
Nothing can beat that.

00:53:20.450 --> 00:53:22.670
Whatever we say before,
it doesn't matter.

00:53:22.670 --> 00:53:24.900
So I did want to take
the opportunity to thank

00:53:24.900 --> 00:53:26.672
Ravi and the Home Depot.

00:53:26.672 --> 00:53:28.130
They have been
phenomenal partners.

00:53:28.130 --> 00:53:29.755
And like he said,
they learned from us,

00:53:29.755 --> 00:53:31.880
but we learned a whole
lot more from them,

00:53:31.880 --> 00:53:34.100
especially on the pain
points of enterprises

00:53:34.100 --> 00:53:36.110
and then how to make
sure that Google Cloud is

00:53:36.110 --> 00:53:38.990
the cloud for enterprises
and cloud natives.

00:53:38.990 --> 00:53:44.330
In the same train, we wanted to
actually end this presentation

00:53:44.330 --> 00:53:45.590
with some action.

00:53:45.590 --> 00:53:49.580
So we wanted to give you a sneak
preview at our IPv6 global load

00:53:49.580 --> 00:53:50.780
balancing.

00:53:50.780 --> 00:53:53.750
And with that I'm going to
ask our friends at the back

00:53:53.750 --> 00:53:56.854
to play the demo for us.

00:53:56.854 --> 00:53:57.836
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:53:57.836 --> 00:53:58.870
- Hi, everyone.

00:53:58.870 --> 00:54:00.680
This is Mike Columbus
with Google Cloud,

00:54:00.680 --> 00:54:03.100
and I'm going to take you
through IPv6 integration

00:54:03.100 --> 00:54:05.230
with the Google Cloud
global load balancer.

00:54:05.230 --> 00:54:07.540
Let's now take a look
at our configuration

00:54:07.540 --> 00:54:09.234
for our global load balancer.

00:54:09.234 --> 00:54:10.900
The first thing you'll
notice is that we

00:54:10.900 --> 00:54:14.110
have a single IPv4 address
that's reachable via anycast

00:54:14.110 --> 00:54:16.000
to Google's points of presence.

00:54:16.000 --> 00:54:18.040
Since the load balancer
is a global entity,

00:54:18.040 --> 00:54:20.980
we can also see that we have two
backends in different regions

00:54:20.980 --> 00:54:22.810
within Google Cloud Platform.

00:54:22.810 --> 00:54:27.310
The first, europe-west1,
the second, us-east1.

00:54:27.310 --> 00:54:28.810
Both instance groups
are configured

00:54:28.810 --> 00:54:31.330
for three instances
and will auto scale

00:54:31.330 --> 00:54:33.850
based on load balancer usage.

00:54:33.850 --> 00:54:37.360
We now have a new requirement
to add IPv6 termination

00:54:37.360 --> 00:54:39.400
on our global load balancer.

00:54:39.400 --> 00:54:41.050
Let's show you how
that's configured.

00:54:41.050 --> 00:54:43.576
First, we'll go into our
external IP addresses,

00:54:43.576 --> 00:54:44.950
and we can see
that we've already

00:54:44.950 --> 00:54:47.500
reserved an IPv6 address.

00:54:47.500 --> 00:54:50.020
Let's now add this to our
load balancer frontend

00:54:50.020 --> 00:54:52.760
by editing its configuration.

00:54:52.760 --> 00:54:55.230
We'll select frontend
configuration

00:54:55.230 --> 00:55:00.652
and add our reserved IPv6
address to the load balancer.

00:55:00.652 --> 00:55:04.670
We'll select done and update.

00:55:04.670 --> 00:55:06.860
While we're waiting
for our IPv6 address

00:55:06.860 --> 00:55:08.720
to be attached to
our load balancer,

00:55:08.720 --> 00:55:11.570
let's go into Cloud DNS and
look at our manage zones.

00:55:11.570 --> 00:55:14.786
We can see we have a zone
for gcpnetworking.com.

00:55:14.786 --> 00:55:18.320
Let's add an IPv6
record to this zone.

00:55:18.320 --> 00:55:24.425
We'll do this by adding a record
set for www.gcpnetworking.com

00:55:24.425 --> 00:55:29.060
of type quad A, and we'll
paste in our IPv6 address

00:55:29.060 --> 00:55:30.600
for our load balancer.

00:55:30.600 --> 00:55:33.590
We'll then click create.

00:55:33.590 --> 00:55:35.120
With our records
in place, let's now

00:55:35.120 --> 00:55:38.230
connect to
www.gcpnetworking.com.

00:55:38.230 --> 00:55:42.856
You can see we have both
IPv4 and v6 records in place.

00:55:42.856 --> 00:55:44.480
We'll connect to the
site using Chrome.

00:55:48.030 --> 00:55:50.580
Our website has been configured
to provide us information

00:55:50.580 --> 00:55:52.119
about the connection.

00:55:52.119 --> 00:55:54.160
The first thing you see
is our client IP address,

00:55:54.160 --> 00:55:56.040
which is an IPv6 address.

00:55:56.040 --> 00:55:57.990
The client then connects
to our load balancer,

00:55:57.990 --> 00:56:01.500
which is also an IPv6
address, as you can see here.

00:56:01.500 --> 00:56:05.340
The load balancer then proxies
this connection to IPv4,

00:56:05.340 --> 00:56:09.450
to our backend, which
lives in us-east1-d.

00:56:09.450 --> 00:56:11.310
In this connection,
I was load balanced

00:56:11.310 --> 00:56:14.160
to a backend in US
east, which makes sense

00:56:14.160 --> 00:56:15.510
since I'm from Boston.

00:56:15.510 --> 00:56:18.240
But how would this look for
a client based in Europe?

00:56:18.240 --> 00:56:21.120
Let's remote into a machine
in Europe to find out.

00:56:21.120 --> 00:56:25.725
Here, we'll connect again
to www.gcpnetworking.com.

00:56:25.725 --> 00:56:27.450
When I connect to
the site, you can

00:56:27.450 --> 00:56:30.750
see that I'm load balanced
to a Europe backend,

00:56:30.750 --> 00:56:34.110
and you can see that this
one lives in europe-west1-b.

00:56:34.110 --> 00:56:35.670
Thank you for your time today.

00:56:35.670 --> 00:56:37.470
You can see on Google
Cloud Platform,

00:56:37.470 --> 00:56:40.380
we've configured a globally
available IPv6 load balance

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:43.360
and solution using anycast
in about the time it

00:56:43.360 --> 00:56:44.610
takes to make a cup of coffee.

00:56:44.610 --> 00:56:45.956
[APPLAUSE]

00:56:45.956 --> 00:56:47.580
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So
you're the first set

00:56:47.580 --> 00:56:49.326
of people to actually
view this demo,

00:56:49.326 --> 00:56:50.700
so thank you for
staying with us.

00:56:50.700 --> 00:56:54.050
[MUSIC PLAYING]

